Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, Jesus' disciples came to him and asked, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He placed the child among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you do not, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever is free of arrogance like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
He who welcomes a little child like him in my name, welcomes me.
But what if someone leads one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to be thrown into the sea with a large millstone tied around his neck.
How terrible it is for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like this must come. But how terrible it is for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life maimed than to have two hands and to be thrown into eternal fire. Where the worm never dies and the fire is not quenched. The cowardly, the faithless, the detestable, the murderers of good men, the sexually immoral, those practicing idolatry, the liars, the murderers, the idolaters, and everyone who loves and practices falsehood – I tell you, all these will be found written in the book of eternal life, and others besides these will be found written in the book of eternal death. The smoke of their torment will rise forever and ever. There will be no rest day or night for those who worship the beast and its image, and for everyone who receives the mark of its name. Blessed and holy are those who have part in the first resurrection. On such, the second death has no power. These will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with Him a thousand years. Blessed and holy are those who have part in the first resurrection. On such, the second death has no power. These will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with Him a thousand years.
Story of the Lost Sheep
Make sure you don't underestimate any of these little ones. That's what I'm telling you. Their angels in heaven can go at any time to visit their Father who is in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Imagine a man who has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray. Will he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the hills? Will he not go after the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If that sheep is found, it will be happier with it than the ninety-nine that did not go astray.
Thus be your father who is in heaven; he does not want to lose one of these little ones. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep the matter between yourselves. If he listens to you, you have regained his affection.
But what if he will not listen to you? In that case, take one or two more persons along with you. The writing says: "Every matter must be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Exodus 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Say that to the church. And what if he even refuses to listen to the church? Do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is true. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here's what I say: Imagine two of you on Earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in Heaven will do it for you.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there with them." The merciless servant.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered and said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to settle all the debts his servants owed him.
And as the king began to do that, a man who owed millions of dollars in debt was brought to him.
The man could not pay his debt, so his master issued an order. The man, his wife, their children, and all their possessions were to be sold to pay off his debt.
The servant knelt before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
His master felt sorry for him. He forgave him for the debt he owed and set him free.
But that server went out and found another server who owed him a few dollars. He grabbed him and started choking him. "Pay me what you owe me!" he said.
The other slave fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. The man would be held there until he could repay what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened, and it greatly disturbed them. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant and said to him, 'You wicked servant! I forgave you all that debt because you pleaded with me.
Haven't you had to show mercy to the other servant just as you had mercy on me?
In his anger, his master handed him over to the guards. He will be punished until he pays all that he owes.
Thus will the heavenly Father deal with you all, if you do not forgive your brother from your hearts.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
The man said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cling to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. So what God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command that a man should give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart, but it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I say to you: Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman, commits adultery. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who are able to accept it are those whom He has helped to live without marriage.
Some men cannot be fathers because they were born that way. Some were made that way by others. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living this way, must do so." The little children were brought to Jesus
Some people brought their little children to Jesus, and they wanted him to lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus placed his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one good God. If you want to enter the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus answered, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony."
Honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And "love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments. What must I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I tell you is true. For it is hard for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven.
And I tell you again, it is difficult for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. And they asked, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals it is impossible, but for God all things are possible."
Peter answered and said, "We have left everything behind to follow you! So what reward will be given to us?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true. When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then, you who have followed me will sit on twelve thrones; and you will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Whoever forsakes his own houses or his own family, or his own fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.
But many of the first will be last, and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who owned a vineyard. He went out early in the morning to hire workers to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them their usual wage for a day of work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market square doing nothing.
And he said to them, "Go you also and work in my vineyard. I will give you your hire as is meet."
He's gone. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and saw others still standing around the place. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long without doing anything?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. And he said to them, "You also go into the vineyard and whatever is right you will receive."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers, saying, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Begin with the last ones who were hired, and then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived, and each received their usual daily wage.
When the first hired hand showed up, they expected to get paid more. But each one of them also got their usual day's wage.
When they received that, they began to complain about the owner.
They said, "These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour." "You paid them the same as you paid us." "We did most of the work and stayed in the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them, saying, "My friend, I am treating you justly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary that you gave them.
Don't I have the right to do whatever I want with my own money? Do you feel deceived because I gave others so generously?
Those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus tells Mary again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem. The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. They will mock him and beat him. They will nail him to a cross. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or ask Jesus a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She fell down on her knees before him and asked him a favor.
What do you want? Jesus asked. "Tell me," she said, "to sit one of my two sons at your right hand in your kingdom. Tell me the other will sit at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking." "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I will drink?" "We can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup; but how and when I am to send you, this is not for me to decide. These places at my right and left are not mine to give; they belong to those for whom my Father in heaven prepares them."
The other ten pupils heard about it and were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Instead, whoever among you wants to be great must be your servant.
And anyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Behold the Son of Man, who came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many." Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men sat by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. He told them to be quiet. But the men cried out even louder, saying, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Sir," they replied, "we want to be seen."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus' Entry into Jerusalem
And they are approaching Jerusalem, and they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. And Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them: "Go into the village that is opposite you, and immediately you will find an ass tied, and a colt with her. Loose them and bring them to me. And if any one say to you, 'Why do you do this?' say, 'The Lord has need of them,' and straightway he will send them." All this was done, that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying: "Tell you the daughter of Zion, Behold, your king comes to you, meek, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass." And the disciples, when Jesus spoke to them, understood that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and the chief priests, and be killed, and on the third day rise again. And Peter took him aside, and began to rebuke him, saying, "God forbid it, Lord! This shall never happen to you." And Jesus turned, and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an offense to me, for you are not mindful of the things that are of God, but of the things that are of men." And Jesus went out and departed from the temple, and his disciples came to him, saying, "Show us the place where you are staying." And Jesus said to them, "Come, and you will see." And they came and saw where Jesus was staying, and they stayed with him that day, for it was about the tenth hour. And Jesus went forth, and his disciples with him, and he came to the fig tree which he had seen, and behold, it had no figs, and he said to it, "No man eat fruit from you hereafter forever." And his disciples heard it. And they came to Jerusalem, and Jesus entered the temple, and he cast out all who were selling and buying in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the seats of those who sold doves, and he said to them, "It is written, My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of thieves." And the scribes and the chief priests sought to lay hands on him, for they feared the people, for all the people were astonished at his teaching. And when Jesus perceived that they would come to take him by force, and to make him king, he sent one of his disciples to the chief priests, saying, "Tell this to the chief priests: 'Look, this is what I told you: While you are still in the cities of Israel, the Son of Man will be betrayed into the hands of sinners.'" And Jesus said to them, "You will all fall away from me this night, for it is written, I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered. But after I am raised up from the dead, I will go before you into Galilee." And Peter said to him, "Even if all are made to fall away from you by night, I will never fall away from you." And Jesus said to him, "Truly, I say to you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times." But he said more emphatically, "If it were necessary for me to die with you, I would not deny you." And they all said, "We, too, will not deny you." And they went to a house, and Jesus began to teach them. And Peter and John and James were reclining with him, and he said to them, "
He said to them, "Go to the village that is in front of you. As soon as you reach it, you will find a she-donkey tied up. It will have its young camel with it. Untie it and bring it."
If anyone tells you anything, say that the Lord needs them. Their owner will send for them immediately."
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. He says:
Say to the daughter of Zion, "Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle and riding on a donkey, on a colt of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
Bring the donkey and the day horse. They threw their cloak on them. Then Jesus sat on the cloaks.
A very large crowd gathered and spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple courts. He began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples."'" (Isaiah 56: 7). "But you are making it into 'a den of robbers.'" (Jeremiah 7: 11)
The blind and lame came to Jesus at the temple, and he healed them there.
The priests, the chief priests, and the legal teachers saw the wonderful things he was doing. They also saw the children in the temple court shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When the chief priests and teachers saw this, they became furious.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures? It says, 'From the lips of children and infants you have ordained praise.'"(Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the people and walked out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. And the fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He came up to it and found nothing on it but leaves. And he said to it, "May you never bear fruit!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. And they asked, "How did the tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered and said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. You must have faith and not doubt. Then you will be able to do what was done to the fig tree. And you can say to the mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." "His authority is questioned"
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered and said, "I will also ask you a question. If you answer me, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven, or from men? And they discussed it among themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, saying, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Then, I will also not tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons, and he went to the first son and said to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I will not," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing. The son replied, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The tax collectors and prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you how to live properly. And you didn't believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. I saw that. But even then you didn't turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who owned some land, so he planted a vineyard. He put up a wall around it. He dug a trench for a wine press inside it. And he also built a watchtower. He let out the vineyard to some farmers. Then he left on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he commanded his servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his slaves. And they beat one of them. And they killed another. And they threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated the servants in the same way.
In the end, he sent his son to them. And he said, "They will deal with my son with respect."
But the tenants saw the Son coming. And they said to one another, "This is the Heir! Come, let's kill him and take his inheritance."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do with those tenants?
"The wicked people will be destroyed," they answered. "Then the vineyard will be let out to other farmers. They will give him his share of the crop at the time of the harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was spoken to you by God, saying, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is the Lord's doing; it is marvelous in our eyes'"? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)
Here is what I say: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to people who will produce its fruit.
Anyone who falls on that stone will be crushed into pieces. But the stone will press on anyone who falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowds. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them other stories, saying:
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding dinner for his son.
He sent his servant to the guests invited for dinner. The servants told them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent some other servants. "Tell the guests that my dinner is ready. I have slaughtered my oxen and my finest livestock. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest of them seized his slaves, treating them harshly and eventually killing them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed the murderers and burned their city.
The king said to his servant, "The wedding feast has been prepared. But the guests were not worthy of attendance."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to visit the guests. He noticed a man there who was not dressed in wedding clothes.
My friend, how did you get in here without a wedding costume? The man couldn't think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servant, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will groan and grit their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen." Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and schemed to entrap Jesus in his words.
They sent their followers to him. And they sent with them some Herodians, saying, "Teacher, we know that you are a good man. And you truly teach the way of God. And you do not allow others to tell you what to do or say. And you do not care how important they are."
So then tell us, what do you think? Is it right that we pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intentions. And he said, "You frauds! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." "Bring him a silver coin."
He asked them: "Whose image is this? And whose are these words?"
"Caesar," they answered. Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On that same day, Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. For they do not believe in the resurrection of the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry his widow. He must have children who will carry on his brother's name.
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. This continued until it reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? She was married to all seven.
Jesus answered, saying, "You err, not knowing the Scriptures, nor the power of God.
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will men give their daughters in marriage to them. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the resurrection of the dead? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at what he taught. The most important commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees were unable to give a reply to Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
And one of them, who was a scholar of the law, tested Jesus with a question.
"My teacher," he asked, "what is the most important commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered, saying, "Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul. Love him with all your mind" (Deuteronomy 6: 5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And equality is: "Love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18)
All that was written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two commandments. Who is the Son of Christ?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them, saying,
What do you think about the Messiah? Who is the son of whom?" "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "my Lord," how could he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any other question.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
He said, "The legal scholars and Pharisees sit on Moses' seat."
So you have to obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They burden themselves with carrying heavy weights and placing them on the shoulders of others, but they do not wish to make any effort to transport them themselves.
All they do is done to be seen by others. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing verses from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the ribbons on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to get the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into city markets. They like people to call them "rabbi".
But do not call anyone on earth 'Rabbi.' For you have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth "father". For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
No one should call you "Master." You have one master, the Christ.
The greatest among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.
How dreadful it will be for you, you teachers of religious law and you Pharisees! You are in for trouble! You claim! You are the ones who shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those who wish to enter to do so. And you will not let those enter who are carrying burdens, even into a room above the inn.
<.>
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! You travel over land and sea to make a single convert, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
"Ah, what sorrow awaits you, blind guides! You say, 'If anyone takes an oath by the temple, it means nothing. But if someone swears by the gold of the temple, they must keep their oath.' "
You are blind and naive! What is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say: "If anyone swears by the name of the altar, he means nothing." But whoever swears by the name of the gift of altar, he is bound to keep his oath."
O blind one! What is more important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar that makes the gift sacred?
For anyone who swears by the name of the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And everyone who swears by the name of the temple, swears by the name of the temple and by the one who dwells in it.
And whoever swears by the name of heaven has sworn by the name of the throne of God and by Him who sits upon it.
How terrible you teachers of the law and Pharisees are! You are hypocrites! You give a tenth of your spices, such as mint, dill, and cumin. But you have not practiced the more important matters of the law, such as justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the lesser matters without neglecting the more important ones.
O ye guides, ye blind! Ye remove the smallest insect from your food; but ye swallow camels!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of decay and dead bones on the inside. You, outwardly, appear righteous to people, but inwardly, you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
Blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the dishes; then the outside will also be clean.
Oh, what a terrible thing this is for you, you religious scholars and Pharisees! You counterfeits! You are like whitewashed tombs. They look beautiful on the outside. But on the inside, they are full of the bones of the dead. They are also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
This applies to you as well. From the outside, you appear to be doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are filled with what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Alas for you, you scholars of the law and you Pharisees, you hypocrites! You build the tombs of the prophets and you decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And she says, "If we had lived in the days of those who came before us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You are bearing witness against yourself. You are admitting that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete the sins of those who preceded you, who lived before you!
You are a nest of poisonous snakes! How will you survive being sentenced to hell?
Therefore, I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, crucify some of them, flog some of them in your synagogues, and persecute them from city to city.
