A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, "I will send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight" (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. For God will forgive them.
All the people from the region of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him. When they confessed their sins, he baptized them in the Jordan River.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach: "After me will come one who is more powerful than I am. I am not worthy to even stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. And he was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
Jesus came up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the heavens torn apart. And he saw the Holy Spirit descend on him like a dove.
He heard a voice calling to him from the sky, saying, "My son, I love you, and I am very pleased with you."
Immediately the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. There the devil assassinated him. Wild animals did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose his first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee. And he began to preach the gospel of God.
He said, "The time has come. The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking along the shore of Lake Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets immediately and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further. As he walked, he saw James and John, the sons of Zebedee, and their brother Andrew. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. And they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. And they went after Jesus. Jesus Expels an Evil Demon
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. On the Sabbath he entered the synagogue and began to teach.
People were amazed at his teaching, for he taught them as one who had authority, not as the teachers of the law.
At that moment, a man cried out in their synagogue. He was under the control of an evil spirit. He said:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One, the Son of God!
Jesus firmly said, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The devil violently shook the man. Then he came out of him with a yell.
Everyone was astonished. They asked one another, "What is this? These are new instructions! And with great authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey his orders."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the temple. Then they went immediately with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her up. The fever left her body. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all the sick and all those who were possessed by demons.
All the people of the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also drove out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was early in the morning and the darkness was still there. Jesus got up and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the neighboring villages. I must preach there also. For this reason I have come."
So he traveled everywhere, in every great and marvelous thing, and taught in their assemblies, and also drove out demons. Jesus heals a man who was afflicted with skin diseases.
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and asked Jesus, saying, "If you are willing to make me clean, you are able to do it."
Jesus filled his deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am ready to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately the illness left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent the man away and gave him a strong warning.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make sacrifices as Moses commanded. This will be proof to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and immediately began to speak. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city publicly. He stayed outside in deserted places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus returned to Capernaum. The people heard that he had returned to his own town.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was no more room even outside the door. And Jesus taught the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get him close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith, and said unto the man, "Son, thy sins be forgiven thee."
There were some law professors sitting there. They were thinking,
Why does this man speak in this way? He is saying something very wicked! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately, Jesus knew what they were thinking in their hearts. And he said to them, "Why do you think these things?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He told me, "Get up. Take your carpet and go home."
The man stood up, took his mat, and then walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
And that time, Jesus began to go out to the edge of the Sea of Galilee. And a large multitude came to Him. And He began to teach them.
And as he walked, he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax booth. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi rose and followed Jesus.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them, "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick. I did not come to call those who think they are at peace with God, but sinners." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees would fast. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John and the Pharisees fast. But your disciples are not fasting. Why are they not fasting?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; in that day they will go out without food.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they did, the new piece would pull away from the old. This would make the holes worse.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath day
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grain fields. The disciples began to pull out some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do what is unlawful on the Sabbath?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the sacred bread. Only the priests were permitted to eat it. David also gave some to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for humankind, and not humankind for the Sabbath."
"For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day."
Healing on Saturday
At another time, Jesus entered the temple. And there was a man who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus' words. They were closely watching him. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak and deformed hand. "Stand up in front of everyone," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what to do on the Sabbath? Is it to do good or to do evil? Is it to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger. He was very angry because their hearts were stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and started to plot with Herod. They wanted to kill Jesus. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd from Galilee followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from gathering around him.
Jesus healed many people. So, the sick were pushed towards him to touch him.
When the people saw those possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
And he struck ten of them, appointing them as messengers, and said to them: "From now on, henceforth, you will be with me. And I will send you out as well to teach."
They will have the authority to expel demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve, and Simon was among them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was Jacob, son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges, which means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus, and Simon the Canaanite.
Judah Iscariot was also one of them. He was the one who later handed Jesus over to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebul
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. They went to take care of him. They said, "He has lost his mind."
There were some professors of the law there. They came from Jerusalem. "He is in the power of Beelzebul," they said. "He is driving out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus called his disciples to him and spoke to them in parables. And he said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
